{#- Auto-generated SWAG default (catch-all) vhost config.
    Source: deploy_swag_configs.yaml. Do not edit manually — changes are overwritten
    on next deploy (edit templates/swag_default.conf.j2 instead).

    Handles any request whose Host header doesn't match a known subdomain (the bare
    domain, typos, or internet-wide vulnerability scanners probing IPs directly).
    Bounces unmatched requests to a ~214MB video (a bot tarpit, backed by the
    `rickroll` container on port 7344) as a deterrent.

    Root-caused 2026-07-27 (see project_crowdsec_iac.md): this rewrite worked too
    well — CrowdSec's nginx bouncer defaults `ENABLE_INTERNAL=false`, which makes it
    skip the ban check entirely (`ngx.exit(ngx.DECLINED)`) for any request nginx
    marks "internal". nginx marks a request internal after ANY `rewrite` fires
    (confirmed true for both `last` and `break`, so it's not a redirect-flag
    quirk) — so every request hitting this vhost's rewrite silently bypassed
    CrowdSec bans entirely, regardless of ban status. This isn't a CrowdSec bug in
    the sense of being broken; ENABLE_INTERNAL defaulting to false is a deliberate
    (if surprising) choice to avoid re-checking nginx's own internal subrequests —
    it just doesn't distinguish "internal" in that sense from "externally-originated
    request that happened to hit a rewrite," which is what this vhost is built on.
    Confirmed via a live A/B test (forged X-Forwarded-For as a known-banned IP
    against this vhost): 200/full-214MB with ENABLE_INTERNAL unset, 403/ban-page
    with it set true — identical request, only that flag changed.

    Fix: set ENABLE_INTERNAL=true bouncer-wide (crowdsec_definitions.yaml /
    templates/crowdsec/crowdsec-nginx-bouncer.conf.j2), not a config change here.
    Checked for side effects: no other site-conf in this repo uses `rewrite`, and
    the only other internal-redirect pattern in use (smdion_errorpages.conf's 502
    error page) is harmless under this flag — the original client already passed
    the ban check before nginx ever reaches the backend and gets a 502, so the
    internal error-page redirect is a redundant, harmless re-check, not a new one. -#}
## Managed by homelab-ops (deploy_swag_configs.yaml) — see templates/swag_default.conf.j2

server {
    listen 80 default_server;
    listen [::]:80 default_server;

    location / {
        return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
    }
}

server {
    listen 8081 ssl default_server;
    listen 443 ssl default_server;
    listen [::]:443 ssl default_server;

    server_name _;

    include /config/nginx/ssl.conf;
    include /config/nginx/includes/maxmind.conf;
    include /config/nginx/includes/smdion_errorpages.conf;

    rewrite ^\/[^/]+(.*)+$ /video.mp4 last;

    root /config/www;
    index index.html index.htm index.php;

    location / {
        include /config/nginx/proxy.conf;
        set $upstream_app 10.10.10.51;
        set $upstream_port 7344;
        set $upstream_proto http;
        proxy_pass $upstream_proto://$upstream_app:$upstream_port;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    }

    # deny access to .htaccess/.htpasswd files
    location ~ /\.ht {
        deny all;
    }
}
